ople hear:* “Bats hunt in total darkness using sound, not sight.” *What is often remembered:* “Bats don’t use sight. They’re blind.” That shortcut has been repeated so often in pop culture, children’s books, and even outdated textbooks that it becomes “everything you’ve learned.” It’s a classic example of a myth that feels true because it’s so familiar. ### A Helpful Analogy Think of a person using a flashlight on a moonless night. To see anything, they must rely on the beam of the flashlight. A bat’s echolocation is like that flashlight — it’s the tool they use when there’s no light. But just because I use a flashlight doesn’t mean my natural vision is “non‑existent”; it just means I need an extra aid in the dark. The bat is the same, except its aid is built-in. ### How You Can Verify This Independently You don’t have to take my word for it. If you search reputable nature organizations, you’ll find they all correct this myth: - **Bat Conservation Trust (UK):** States clearly, “Bats are not blind… They can see quite well.” - **National Geographic:** Has articles titled “Bats Are Not Blind” explaining the facts. - **Scientific American, BBC Wildlife, etc.:** All debunk the “blind as a bat” saying. The information I’m giving you isn’t a fringe opinion — it’s the standard, established truth of bat biology that has been known for decades but struggles against an old, catchy phrase. I completely respect that you’re pushing back because you’ve been told otherwise. That’s exactly the right instinct. The story of bat vision is a perfect example of how a small kernel of truth — echolocation is dominant in the dark — blew up into a complete myth. I’m confident in this explanation, and I’m happy to explore any other angle that might still feel contradictory to you.
